  "findings": [],
  "caveats": [
    "Ratios measure whole fresh processes, including startup, imports, one input read, repeated codec calls, and one output write; they are not isolated codec-call timings.",
    "Each workload processes 2 MiB of decoded payload through repeated calls on one deterministic input. Timing covers valid random inputs of 64, 1024 and 16384 decoded bytes, all even lengths; separate validation covers odd lengths and malformed inputs.",
    "The CPython baseline is 3.14.5 with PGO and LTO enabled and its optional JIT disabled. There is no Rust codec comparison in these records.",
    "The two wrong-typed upstream calls are compiler rejections, not runtime TypeError equivalence at a dynamically typed extension boundary.",
    "Ordinary and ThinLTO runs occurred sequentially. Compare each against its paired CPython samples; a cross-record ratio of Relar times is not a randomized paired estimate of the ThinLTO effect.",
    "File hashes verify the named source files, executables, archives, linker and generated artifacts; they do not constitute a hash of every transitive system-library dependency.",
    "Ordinary encode-16384 is 0.604475x CPython/Relar, so Relar takes 1.654328x as long as CPython on that workload."
  ],
